<section image-layout="blank" id="qPTo9Ra8PL" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="j19AMP_Atx" vertical-align="center" colspan="35"><p role="eyebrow">History detective case · Southern Brazil · 1835–1845</p><display size="lg">Three clues.</display><h1 font-size="42pt">One unfinished story.</h1><p font-size="lg"><b>Tax paper · folded map · letter from home</b></p><p font-size="lg">What can they reveal about the Ragamuffin War, known in Portuguese as the <i>Revolução Farroupilha</i>?</p><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="V4cMI6-6I3" background-color="accent" padding="14pt 18pt"><p role="eyebrow">Detective question</p><h3>Why did it begin—and why did different lives feel it differently?</h3></grid-item></grid></grid-item><grid-item id="B2xnv6g21v" padding="0" colspan="25"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/Yaffpv4CNHlZ4_f4Cqm8R.png" saved-media-id="41xc7hqhvpiodrl" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum-table illustration of three carefully arranged objects from nineteenth-century southern Brazil: a blank tax paper with wax seal, a folded schematic map with no lettering, and a tied letter from home, colored-pencil shading on warm cream paper, postcard red question marks as small paper cutouts, river blue and sun yellow accents, clear daylight, no people, no weapons, no legible text" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>